<mujoco>
    <option
        gravity="0 0 -.5"
    />
    <worldbody>
        <camera
            name="robot_camera"
            pos="2 -2 2"
        />

        <geom
            type="plane"
            size="5 5 0.1"
            rgba="0.969 0.969 0.898 1"
        />

        <body
            name="arm"
            pos="1 0 0"
        >
            <joint
                name="hinge"
                type="hinge"
                axis="1 0 0"
            />
            <!-- without the geom the body (which is just am arm) will not have inertia so add geom
            which is a physical object in the simulator to automatically add mass/inertia to the
            body else you can define it directly but it's best to use geom-->
            <geom
                type="capsule"
                fromto="0 0 0 0 0 1"
                size="0.05"
                density="1000"
            />
        </body>
    </worldbody>

    <actuator>
        <motor
            joint="hinge"
            gear="50" />
    </actuator>

    <sensor>
        <jointpos
            name="arm_angle"
            joint="hinge"
        />
    </sensor>
</mujoco>

<!-- 
Understanding qpos
For example, for an arm with one hinge joint

       |
       O  << hinge joint
      /
     /

If the arm rotates 30 degs, the simulator stores the data in qpos. So qpos is the robot's memory of
where every joint is currently at.

      O  <- shoulder
     /
    O   <- elbow
   /

print(data.qpos) will print an array rep radians, for example [1.5] which is the position of a
single hinge joint or [0.5 1.0] for two hinge joints

print(data.qpos[0]) returns, in radians, the postition of hinge joint 1 and so on as usual

You can also define a position to a joint by simply assigning it to a number of your choice

The meaning of each entry in qpos depends on the type of joint:
- Hinge joint (type="hinge") → angle in radians
- Slide joint (type="slide") → linear distance (usually meters)
- Free joint (type="free") → 7 values (3 for position + 4 for orientation as a quaternion)

What is a controller?
A controller is a piece of code that decides: given the robot's current state what should the
robot's motors do?
Observe      Speedometer says 30mph
↓                      ↓
Think          "I want to go 40mph"
↓                      ↓
Act             Press gas pedal

data.ctrl[0] = 1 this controls the motor, keeps pushing w the force of 1 forever. But irl u want
something smarter, not just pushing at the same force forever

For example:
Where is the arm?
↓
Is it below 45°?
↓
Yes
↓
Push
Otherwise
↓
Stop pushing


-->